     "result_text_tail": "The pilot title was too absolute: the pilot does fit at 60% growth.\n  - The cash gap was measured on a different basis from the software case. It now uses the same falling-burn basis.\n  - The reopen gate was too lenient and now requires a site to make money after its fixed costs.\n  - A cheaper option is now tested: running the pop-up six more months.\n\n**Choices I made because you weren't available to ask (all noted in the deck)**\n- **Format and look:** a deep, 43-slide deck for a board approve-or-reject decision, structured as a client diagnostic, in the navy theme.\n- **Assumptions I set:** stores open 365 days a year; a $1-an-hour step to test rider pay rules; stress cases at 40% and 30% software growth.\n- **Software cash model:** costs stay flat apart from the four hires the pack names, and revenue equals ARR. The five simplifications are listed in the appendix for the CFO to confirm.\n- **Store-based delivery for grocers:** recommended for scoping but left out of the cash model, because the pack gives no price or cost for it.\n- **Where the pack is silent:** the date of the $12.0M is not given, so both cases start from it in January 2027. If it is two months of burn lower, 40% growth still holds with about $0.5M to spare. The pack also has no city-level competitor data, so the \"nobody owns the Midwest\" claim is listed as not pursued.\n\nPossible next steps: switch the theme, or tighten the software cash model once the CFO confirms the cost base and the date of the cash figure."
